.TH "route" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
route \- setup the route table entries at gRouter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
route show
route add -dev tunN -net network -netmask mask [-gw next_hop]
route del route_number
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B route
command modifies the routing table within the GINI router. It is meant to setup the static
routes within the router: the table is the only thing the router consults to decide where a
packet goes, and no routing protocol fills it in unless you load one (see gpipe cp).

When you press Run, gBuilder creates the router's interfaces (tun1, tun2, ... one per link, in
the order shown by ifconfig show) and adds a route for the subnet on each of them. In a topology
with several routers it also adds routes to the subnets behind the other routers. To reach a
network the table does not cover, add a route for it. In route show, an entry with no next hop
(reached directly on its link) is marked C, connected; one with a next hop is marked S, static.
A routing protocol module may add entries of its own.

.B add
needs the interface the packets should leave by
.RI ( -dev
tun2), the destination network
.RI ( -net )
and its mask
.RI ( -netmask ),
always in that order. Without
.B -gw
the destination is taken to be on that interface's own link and is reached directly. With
.B -gw
the packets are handed to next_hop, a router on that link, to carry further.

When several entries match a destination, the router uses the most specific one (the longest
mask). So a /32 entry for one host beats a /24 for its subnet, which beats the default route.

.B del
removes one entry, by the number shown in the first column of
.BR route
.BR show .
The other entries keep their numbers, so look again before deleting a second one.

Routes added here last until the router stops; the next Run starts again from the routes
gBuilder computes. The Routes tab of the Router Lab adds and deletes routes the same way, and
shows the route command it sends.

.SH EXAMPLES

Reach the subnet 10.0.3.0/24 through the router 10.0.2.2, on the link at tun2:
.RS
.nf
route add -dev tun2 -net 10.0.3.0 -netmask 255.255.255.0 -gw 10.0.2.2
.fi
.RE

A route for one host only (a /32 mask), which overrides the route for its subnet:
.RS
.nf
route add -dev tun1 -net 10.0.3.20 -netmask 255.255.255.255 -gw 10.0.1.5
.fi
.RE

A default route, for every destination nothing else matches. The network and mask are both
0.0.0.0; there is no shorter form:
.RS
.nf
route add -dev tun1 -net 0.0.0.0 -netmask 0.0.0.0 -gw 10.0.1.254
.fi
.RE

Show the table, then delete entry 2:
.RS
.nf
route show
route del 2
.fi
.RE

